.TH SPINDLE-CONFIG-HELPER 1 "November 2026" "Spindle 0.13" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
spindle-config-helper \- generate and validate Spindle configuration files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B spindle-config-helper
[\fB-h\fR|\fB--help\fR] [\fB-V\fR|\fB--version\fR] [\fB--config-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B spindle-config-helper
is a utility for generating Spindle configuration files in HPC
environments. It analyzes the local system to automatically detect optimal
configuration settings and outputs a
.I spindle.conf
file suitable for the environment.
.PP
The tool performs the following analysis:
.IP \(bu 2
Detects local vs. remote filesystem mounts (NFS, Lustre, GPFS, tmpfs, overlay)
.IP \(bu 2
Validates
.B $TMPDIR
availability and recommends appropriate paths
.IP \(bu 2
Tests SSH connectivity between MPI ranks to verify compute node access
.IP \(bu 2
Discovers Python installations via
.B $PYTHONHOME
and Python's
.B sys.prefix
and
.B sys.path
.IP \(bu 2
Generates
.I localprefix
from top-level local directories
.IP \(bu 2
Validates path writability and accessibility
.IP \(bu 2
Tests Munge availability and functionality
.PP
The tool must be run as an MPI program on at least two compute nodes
to provide accurate environment analysis, particularly for SSH connectivity testing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Display version information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-config-help
Display configuration file syntax for all Spindle options and exit.
.SH INSTRUCTIONS
To generate a Spindle configuration file:
.IP 1. 4
Set
.B PYTHONHOME
to the Python installation your programs will use.
.IP 2. 4
Set
.B TMPDIR
to node-local temporary storage (e.g.,
.IR /tmp ).
.IP 3. 4
Run
.B spindle-config-helper
as you would any other parallel program, e.g.:
.RS 4
.nf
flux run -N2 -n2 spindle-config-helper
srun -N2 -n2 spindle-config-helper
.fi
.RE
.IP 4. 4
Review the output, then redirect or copy/paste to
.IR ~/.spindle/spindle.conf .
.PP
See the tool's output for explanations of how it chooses paths and validates the environment.
.SH CONFIGURATION OUTPUT
The tool generates a minimal configuration with four essential path variables:
.TP
.B commpaths
Local directories for inter-process communication. Based on
.B $TMPDIR
if set and valid, otherwise recommends a suitable local filesystem.
.TP
.B cachepaths
Local directories for file caching. Uses similar logic to
.BR commpaths .
.TP
.B python-prefix
Python installation directories discovered via
.B $PYTHONHOME
and Python's
.B sys.prefix
and
.BR sys.path .
Validates that paths contain Python packages.
.TP
.B localprefix
Colon-separated list of top-level directories that should be excluded from
caching. Generated from local (non-remote) filesystem mount points.
Overlay filesystems are treated as non-local.
.PP
The output includes warnings and notes as comments:
.IP \(bu 2
.B WARNING:
Issues that require attention (missing environment variables, failed tests, invalid paths)
.IP \(bu 2
.B Note:
Successful validations and test results
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PYTHONHOME
Python installation prefix. Preferred source for Python path discovery.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Preferred directory for
.B commpaths
and
.BR cachepaths .
If not set or invalid, the tool will issue a warning and attempt to find
suitable alternatives.
.SH SPINDLE OPTIONS
Any valid Spindle configuration option can be passed after
.BR \-\- .
These options are parsed using Spindle's built-in option handling.
Use
.B spindle-config-helper \-\- \-\-usage
to see all available options.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Generate a new configuration to stdout:
.nf
.RS
spindle-config-helper
.RE
.fi
.TP
Generate configuration under MPI:
.nf
.RS
flux run -N2 -n2 spindle-config-helper > ~/.spindle/spindle.conf
srun -N2 -n2 spindle-config-helper | tee spindle.conf
.RE
.fi
.TP
Check warnings in output:
.nf
.RS
spindle-config-helper | grep WARNING
.RE
.fi
.TP
View configuration file syntax for all Spindle options:
.nf
.RS
spindle-config-helper \-\-config-help
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/spindle.conf
System-wide Spindle configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/.spindle/spindle.conf
Per-user Spindle configuration file (recommended location).
.TP
.I /proc/mounts
Used to detect filesystem mount points and types.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. Valid configuration was generated.
.TP
.B 1
Error. Invalid arguments or configuration error occurred.
.SH NOTES
.SS MPI Requirement
The tool must be run as an MPI program on at least two nodes for accurate results.
Running on a single node will issue a warning, as SSH ring testing requires
multiple nodes to properly validate inter-node connectivity.
.SS Path Validation
The tool validates paths by checking:
.IP \(bu 2
Filesystem is local (not NFS, Lustre, GPFS, or overlay)
.IP \(bu 2
Path is writable by the current user
.IP \(bu 2
Parent directories exist
.PP
Paths that fail validation are filtered out and reported in warnings.
.SS SSH Ring Testing
When running under MPI with multiple nodes, each rank tests SSH connectivity
to the next rank in a ring pattern (highest rank connects to rank 0).
This verifies that compute nodes can communicate via SSH without password prompts.
.SS Python Discovery
Python installations are discovered in order of preference:
.IP 1. 4
.B $PYTHONHOME
environment variable
.IP 2. 4
Python's
.B sys.prefix
(queried from Python itself)
.IP 3. 4
Python's
.B sys.path
entries
.PP
The tool validates that discovered paths contain actual Python packages,
not just the Python binary.
.SS Overlay Filesystems
Overlay filesystems (detected via
.IR /proc/mounts )
are treated as non-local and excluded from
.BR commpaths ,
.BR cachepaths ,
and
.BR localprefix .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR spindle (1)
.PP
Spindle documentation: https://github.com/hpc/Spindle
